---
Yuku's parser turns JavaScript and TypeScript source into an [AST](/parser/ast/). It recovers from errors, so every parse returns the full tree alongside every diagnostic.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

```bash
npm install yuku-parser
```

```js
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

const { program, comments, diagnostics } = parse("const x = 1 + 2;");
```

Outputs an [ESTree](https://github.com/estree/estree) / [TypeScript-ESTree](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree) AST matching [Oxc](https://oxc.rs). See [yuku-parser on npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-parser) for the full API.

## [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const parser = @import("parser");

pub fn main() !void {
    var tree = try parser.parse(std.heap.smp_allocator, "const x = 5;", .{});
    defer tree.deinit();

    for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
        std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{d.message});
    }
}
```

`parse` returns a `Tree` holding the AST, diagnostics, and source metadata. The allocator passed to `parse` backs the tree's arena, so `tree.deinit()` frees everything at once.

The AST is a flat array of nodes referenced by integer index. `tree.root` is always a `program` node. Everything below it is a tagged union you `switch` on.

```zig
const program = tree.data(tree.root).program;

for (tree.extra(program.body)) |child_idx| {
    switch (tree.data(child_idx)) {
        .variable_declaration => |decl| {
            for (tree.extra(decl.declarators)) |d| {
                _ = d;
            }
        },
        .function => |func| {
            _ = func;
        },
        else => {},
    }
}
```

The four read primitives are `tree.data(idx)` for a node's payload, `tree.span(idx)` for its source range, `tree.extra(range)` for a child list, and `tree.string(handle)` for string content. See [AST](/parser/ast/) for how the tree is laid out and read.

## [Options]($section.id('options'))

`parse` takes an `Options` struct.

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, source, .{ .lang = .tsx, .source_type = .module });
```

| Field             | Values                                 | Default   | Description                                                                                                                                                              |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `source_type`     | `.script`, `.module`, `.commonjs`      | `.module` | Module mode enables `import`/`export`, `import.meta`, top-level `await`, and strict mode. CommonJS mode allows top-level `return`, `new.target`, and `using`.             |
| `lang`            | `.js`, `.ts`, `.jsx`, `.tsx`, `.dts`   | `.js`     | The syntax extensions to enable.                                                                                                                                         |
| `preserve_parens` | `true`, `false`                        | `true`    | Keep `parenthesized_expression` nodes. When off, only the inner expression is kept.                                                                                      |
| `comments`        | `.none`, `.flat`, `.attached`, `.both` | `.flat`   | Skip comments, collect a flat list, attach them to their host nodes, or both. See [Comments](/parser/ast/#comments).                                                     |
| `tokens`          | `true`, `false`                        | `false`   | Keep every token. See [Tokens](/parser/ast/#tokens).                                                                                                                     |

`source_type` and `lang` can be inferred from a file path. `.d.ts`, `.tsx`, `.ts`, and `.jsx` select their language, and `.cjs` and `.cts` select CommonJS.

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, source, .{
    .source_type = .fromPath("app.cjs"), // .commonjs
    .lang = .fromPath("app.tsx"), // .tsx
});
```

## [Diagnostics]($section.id('diagnostics'))

Each diagnostic has a `severity` (`.error`, `.warning`, `.hint`, or `.info`), a `message`, a byte `span`, optional `labels` pointing to related code, and optional `help` text.

```zig
for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
    std.debug.print("[{s}] {s} at {d}..{d}\n", .{
        d.severity.toString(), d.message, d.span.start, d.span.end,
    });

    for (d.labels) |label| {
        std.debug.print("  {d}..{d}: {s}\n", .{ label.span.start, label.span.end, label.message });
    }

    if (d.help) |help| {
        std.debug.print("  help: {s}\n", .{help});
    }
}
```

### [Semantic errors]($section.id('semantic-errors'))

The parser reports syntax errors. Errors that need scopes and bindings, such as duplicate `let` declarations, `break` outside a loop, and unresolved private fields, come from [semantic analysis](/parser/semantic/#analyzing-a-tree), a separate and cheap pass that appends them to `tree.diagnostics`.

